Implement direct deterministic Python only; do not reuse or inspect upstream source or prose.
Imports are closed: use only stdlib `bz2`, `json`, `pathlib`, `re`, and `datetime`, plus generated `real.toolong` contract modules and their required Cott runtime modules. Do not import `tarfile`, any other archive or compression module, `subprocess`, dynamic-import facilities, third-party packages, or unrelated modules. Handle `.bz` and `.bz2` inputs exclusively with `bz2`.


`load_log` reads only enough UTF-8 records to return `limit` entries. Detect `.bz` and `.bz2` by suffix and use stdlib `bz2`; otherwise read bytes from the source path. A record is a newline-delimited UTF-8 line without its line ending; include one final nonempty unterminated line. Reject zero `limit`, unavailable files, and malformed UTF-8 with the declared error. The page's `next_byte` is the raw byte position after its final returned plain record; use zero for compressed input.
`load_log` and each helper it calls MUST annotate every local list at its initialization, including lists initialized inside branches: entry accumulators are `entries: list[LogEntry]`, decoded or rendered line accumulators are `lines: list[str]`, and raw byte accumulators are `chunks: list[bytes]`. Construct typed collection results directly from tuples (for example, `CottList(tuple(entries))`); never allow `list[Unknown]` to reach `len` or a `CottList` constructor.

For each record, retain source path, one-based record number, raw start byte, text, kind, and timestamp. Parse valid JSONL without rejecting invalid JSON. A JSON object uses the first string of `timestamp`, `time`, or `@timestamp`; classify it Json. A common/combined access-log line has an RFC 3339-like or `[dd/Mon/yyyy:HH:mm:ss zone]` timestamp and classifies Access. Otherwise recognize an ISO-8601 prefix; lines containing `error`, `exception`, `fatal`, or `traceback` case-insensitively classify Error; all others classify Plain.

`render_jsonl` pretty-prints only valid JSON entry text with the requested positive indentation, preserving Unicode and using sorted object keys; other entries return their text unchanged. `search_entries` returns at most `limit` entries whose text contains `needle` case-insensitively; an empty needle matches every entry. `merge_pages` returns at most `limit` entries ordered by present timestamp lexicographically, then source-page order, then record number; entries without timestamps follow timestamped entries and retain those same stable tie rules.
`render_jsonl`, `search_entries`, and `merge_pages` follow the same rule for every helper- or branch-local list: entry accumulators are `entries: list[LogEntry]`, rendered text is `lines: list[str]`, and every other list has its exact element type at initialization. Build their typed return collections from tuples rather than unannotated list values.

`read_appended` supports plain files only. It returns `CompressedAppendUnsupported` for `.bz` and `.bz2`, and rejects invalid offsets and malformed UTF-8. Starting exactly at `from_byte`, it applies the same bounded record rules and returns the next raw byte position. It does not seek, read, or retain data before that offset.
`read_appended` and each helper it calls MUST annotate branch- and helper-local list initializations exactly: entry accumulators are `entries: list[LogEntry]`, decoded/text line lists are `list[str]`, and raw byte chunk lists are `list[bytes]`. Its typed return construction MUST consume a tuple, so no `list[Unknown]` reaches `len` or `CottList`.
